  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 382,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 181,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56519,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 181,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microglandular adenosis occurs in the breast.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 383,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 182,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56520,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 182,
        "headingText": "<p>Microglandular adenosis is often diagnosed incidentally in breast biopsies performed for other reasons. It may also form a mass {21847399} or present as a mammographic density or occasionally as mammographically detected calcifications. Carcinomas arising in microglandular adenosis have presented with a palpable mass {25337263}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-05T19:25:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 384,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 183,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56521,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 183,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microglandular adenosis has been reported in women over a wide age range.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 385,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 184,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56522,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 184,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>No specific factors have been identified that are etiologically linked to microglandular adenosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 386,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 185,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56523,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 185,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Several array comparative genomic hybridization&ndash;based analyses showed that progressive molecular changes were present in microglandular adenosis and associated invasive carcinomas. The acquisition of genetic alterations was identified in the progression from microglandular adenosis to the synchronous invasive carcinoma component, suggesting a clonal evolution. However, these findings are preliminary and additional studies are needed {22486256; 26806567; 27713419}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 393,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 206,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56524,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 206,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microglandular adenosis does not have a characteristic appearance on macroscopic evaluation. It can look like normal breast parenchyma or form a poorly defined nodularity or mass. In cases of carcinoma arising in a background of microglandular adenosis, a grossly evident mass lesion is usually present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 387,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 186,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56525,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 186,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microglandular adenosis is composed of small, round, tubular structures that grow in a haphazard non-lobulocentric pattern {29949742}. The glands are not compressed by surrounding stroma; luminal spaces are open and often contain colloid-like eosinophilic PAS-positive diastase-resistant secretions. Microglandular adenosis glands are uniform and similar in size to or slightly larger than a normal lobular acinus. The glands are composed of a single layer of cuboidal epithelial cells with small nuclei and amphophilic cytoplasm. Myoepithelial cells are absent, but immunohistochemistry or electron microscopy can be used to demonstrate a basement membrane surrounding the glands {29949742; 23584829}. The cells of microglandular adenosis typically express cytokeratins, S100, cathepsin&nbsp;D, and EGFR (HER1), and they are negative for EMA, ER and PR, and ERBB2 (HER2) overexpression by immunohistochemistry {18300793}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 388,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 187,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56526,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 187,
        "headingText": "<p>Cytology shows sparse cellularity and a monotonous population of medium-sized cells with uniform round nuclei, small nucleoli, and vacuolated clear cytoplasm {8458288}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-05T19:26:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 389,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 188,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56527,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 188,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 392,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 191,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56528,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 191,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a haphazard proliferation of small, round, and uniform glands infiltrating into the breast parenchyma and adipose tissue without destroying the pre-existing normal glands; the glands are lined by a single layer of cuboidal cells without atypia.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 390,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 189,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56529,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 189,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 391,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 190,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56530,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 127,
        "headingId": 190,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microglandular adenosis is a benign proliferation, but atypical forms and carcinomas arising in microglandular adenosis have been described {19047897; 12883237}. On the basis of molecular and cytogenetic similarities among these lesions, some investigators have suggested that microglandular adenosis is a non-obligate precursor lesion of basal-type breast carcinoma {22486256}. More studies are needed to establish this connection.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-28T11:21:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]